<!--
    GH-840 (kuna): "A variadic double made from an integer's bits prints as
    the integer".  https://github.com/Noelo-Lab/kuna/issues/840

    Source (each call, jump or branch aimed at a `ret` standing in for pr at 0x100f00):
      int pr(const char *f, ...);
      int b2(long u) { double d; __builtin_memcpy(&d, &u, 8); return pr("%f", d); }
      int c1(long u, long v) { double a, b; __builtin_memcpy(&a, &u, 8);
        __builtin_memcpy(&b, &v, 8); return pr("%f %f %ld", a, b, u); }
      int k1(double d) { return pr("%f", d); }
    b2 is clang -O0 (movsd from the stack slot), b2g is the same source at
    gcc -O2 (movq %rdi,%xmm0), c1 and k1 are gcc -O2 and clang -O0.

    Stage model: P9 ActionSetCasts.  A variadic argument in xmm0/xmm1 has no
    declared parameter, so the cast pass measured it against no type and a
    declared `long` printed as `pr("%f",u)`, which compiles back to an integer
    in rsi.  The argument is now required to be a double when its printed
    type is an integer, and the cast prints as a union bit reinterpretation.
    k1 is the control: a double already, no cast.
-->
